# Variables

## Let's see what this looks like in code:

#### This code defines the variables using the keyword `let`.

#### In JavaScript, a variable must be declared, or defined, before it can be used. 

<p style="text-align:center;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/3fc90531df0a250a6d4d032641296c12-image-1762197748560.png" 
       alt="JavaScript variable example" 
       style="width:600px; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);">
</p>


### Here, an **event listener** increases the `strength` variable by 2 each time the **strength-up** button is clicked.


<p style="text-align:center;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/642d6e6839a3abde291b95a4ae7258f1-image-1771971228529.png" 
       alt="Event handler example" 
       style="width:600px; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);">
</p>

## Remember:

#### There are always two steps when working with variables:

- #### 1. Define the variable (give it a name and a starting value).

- #### 2. Use or update the variable within the program’s actions or logic.


## Let's practice! 




